---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<ManualLayout slug="ai">
  <h2 id="on-device">On your device</h2>
  <p>
    Every AI feature runs on Apple’s <strong>Foundation Models</strong>, on your own Mac, iPhone or
    iPad: <strong>Apple Intelligence</strong> by default, or an open model you download and run on
    your device with <a href="#mlx">MLX</a>. Your notes are processed on the device and are not
    sent to us or to any AI service. There are no third-party AI services and no API keys. The one
    thing that leaves the device is a web search, when <a href="#assistant">the Assistant</a> or
    Research looks something up.
  </p>
  <p>
    A feature is available when the model chosen for it can run. Apple Intelligence needs a device
    that supports it, with Apple Intelligence turned on — in System Settings on the Mac, Settings
    on iPhone and iPad. An MLX model needs to be downloaded, or its folder chosen. Otherwise the AI
    commands are unavailable, and the AI settings say why.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything else in HelloNotes works without them — the AI is an addition, never a requirement.
  </p>

  <h2 id="note-actions">Note actions</h2>
  <p>
    These live in the <strong>Note</strong> menu, beside Rename and Duplicate — not in a panel of
    their own. Each answer appears in the inspector tab that already holds that kind of
    information, so you find it where you would have gone looking anyway. On iPhone and iPad they
    are in the <span aria-hidden="true">✨</span> menu in the toolbar above the note.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Summarise Note</strong> — a short summary of the current note, shown at the top of
      the <strong>Outline</strong> tab. A summary is an outline at a different resolution.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Suggest Tags</strong> — proposes tags based on the content, in the{' '}
      <strong>Tags</strong> tab; you choose which to keep.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Suggest Links</strong> — finds notes that are plausibly related, in the{' '}
      <strong>References</strong> tab beside the backlinks they would join.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rewrite or Expand Note…</strong> — improve, shorten, simplify, restructure or flesh
      out the note. You see the result and choose <em>Replace Note</em> or <em>Insert Below</em>;
      nothing changes until you do.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Selecting text gives you the actions that need your notes to make sense — link the phrase, find
    related notes, explain it using what you have written. On the Mac they appear in a small
    floating bar; on iPhone and iPad they join the standard selection menu. General rewriting is
    left to <strong>Writing Tools</strong>, which the system already puts in every text view.
  </p>
  <p>
    Can’t remember where a command lives? On the Mac, <kbd>⇧⌘P</kbd> opens a
    <strong>command palette</strong>: type a command’s name to run it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="review-links">Review links</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Note ▸ Review Links…</strong> (<kbd>⇧⌘L</kbd>) walks the note phrase by phrase the way
    a spell check does — <strong>Link</strong>, <strong>Skip</strong> or <strong>Never</strong>. You
    see the phrase in its own sentence and the opening lines of the note it would point at, because
    the same phrase can deserve a link in one paragraph and not the next.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every phrase that names another note is a candidate. When a note turns up more than ten, the
      ten whose targets are most related to what you are writing are the ones shown — a long note
      can otherwise produce dozens, and a list nobody finishes reading is a list that gets skipped.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Never</strong> is remembered for that collection, so a phrase you have rejected stops
      coming back. It is kept on your device rather than in your notes — a decision you made is not
      something to commit to a shared repository.
    </li>
    <li>Nothing is written until you finish, and the whole set undoes in one step.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This one needs no model at all: it is an exact scan of your own text.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compose">New note from a prompt</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>File ▸ New Note from a Prompt…</strong> (<kbd>⌃⌘N</kbd>) writes a new note, in two
    modes. <strong>Write</strong> composes one from a description.{' '}
    <strong>Research</strong> investigates a question on the web across several angles and lands the answer as a note, with
    its sources gathered at the end and a record of the question it came from.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Research needs a model with room to read web pages</strong> — at least 8,000 tokens.
    AFM 3 Core Advanced and MLX models have that. Research uses the model chosen for the Assistant,
    and if that one can’t hold enough, the sheet says so rather than starting a run that cannot
    finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    Either way the draft is connected to what you already have: notes you own on the topic are
    offered as link targets, and <strong>every link that comes back is checked against your
    collection</strong>. Anything naming a note that does not exist is turned back into ordinary
    text, and the draft says so. A link that looks real but points nowhere is worse than no link.
  </p>
  <p>
    You read the whole draft — title and body, both editable — before anything is created. Cancel
    leaves nothing behind. The button is <strong>Create Note</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ghost-text">Suggest as I type</h2>
  <p>
    Grey text appears after the cursor when you pause at the end of a line in the{' '}
    <strong>Edit</strong> view. On the Mac, <kbd>⌥⇥</kbd> or <kbd>→</kbd> accepts it and{' '}
    <kbd>Esc</kbd> dismisses it; <kbd>⌥Esc</kbd> or <kbd>F5</kbd> asks for one without waiting.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>The suggestion is not part of your note until you accept it.</strong> It is drawn on
      screen and nothing more — it cannot reach a save, a search result, your links or a Git diff.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    On iPhone and iPad, tap the suggestion to accept it — or press <kbd>⌥⇥</kbd> with a keyboard
    attached; <kbd>Esc</kbd> dismisses it. Suggestions come from the model chosen for the writing
    tools, on your device. Off until you turn it on, in the <strong>Inline Completion</strong>{' '}
    section of the AI settings.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ask">Ask your library</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ask Library</strong> answers questions using your own notes as the source. It finds the
    relevant ones, reads them, and answers with <strong>citations</strong> back to the notes it drew
    on so you can check the reasoning and jump to the original.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is most useful for questions you know you have written about but cannot place — “what did I
    decide about the pricing model?” rather than general knowledge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="assistant">The Assistant</h2>
  <p>
    The Assistant — on the Mac, <strong>View ▸ Assistant</strong> (<kbd>⇧⌘A</kbd>) — is a chat
    window with tools: it can search your collection, read notes, propose edits and search the web.
    It looks at your notes before it answers a question about them, and says what it is doing while
    it works — searching your notes, reading a note, waiting for your approval. The wrench and
    screwdriver button at the top turns its tools off, for a conversation that neither reads nor
    changes your notes.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>Changes wait for your approval.</strong> When the Assistant wants to change a note, it
      shows you the exact change first and waits; nothing is written until you approve it. If you
      choose <strong>Allow all this session</strong>, the rest of that conversation’s changes are
      made without asking — except deleting a note, which always asks.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    If it proposes several changes at once, they wait their turn, one card at a time. A change is
    made only to the text you approved: if the note was edited while the card was waiting, the
    Assistant is told to read it again instead. <strong>Skills</strong> hold reusable instructions
    for repeated jobs — a note named <code>SKILL.md</code>, with a <code>name:</code> and a{' '}
    <code>description:</code> in its front matter — and the Assistant loads the one a request
    matches.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a model with a window of at least 16,000 tokens — in this version, an MLX model on a Mac —
    the Assistant can also read whole web pages and run <strong>deep research</strong>, a longer
    multi-step investigation across your notes and the web. With a smaller window it searches the
    web and works from the results. The model it is using, and where that model runs, is always
    shown at the top of the window.
  </p>

  <h2 id="settings">Choosing a model</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Settings ▸ AI</strong> has two separate choices: the model for the{' '}
    <strong>Assistant</strong> (and the research behind New Note from a Prompt), and the model for
    the <strong>writing tools</strong> — Summarise, Suggest Tags and Links, Rewrite, Compose and
    Ask Library. Each can be:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>On-Device</strong> — Apple Intelligence, named by the model your hardware runs (AFM 3
      Core, or AFM 3 Core Advanced on the most capable Apple silicon). The default.
    </li>
    <li><strong>MLX</strong> — an open model you have downloaded, run on your device.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Under each choice is one line saying where your text goes, and — if the model can’t run right
    now — why not. <strong>Creativity</strong> controls how varied the Assistant’s answers are;
    on a model that reasons, <strong>Thinking</strong> sets how long it considers a hard question.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the Mac this is the <strong>AI</strong> tab of Settings (<kbd>⌘,</kbd>); on iPhone and iPad,{' '}
    <strong>Settings ▸ AI ▸ Models</strong>. The Assistant’s window opens the same screen from its{' '}
    <span aria-hidden="true">⚙︎</span> button.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mlx">Open models with MLX</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>HelloNotes suggests no models.</strong> Choosing one is the advanced end of the app: a
    recommended list is a generation out of date within months, and whether a model is any good at
    your work is between you and the model. Name one, or point at a folder holding one; either way it
    is stored in your device’s caches, not in your notes or your backups. A download can be
    cancelled, and <strong>Remove Download</strong> gives the space back.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Any MLX model on Hugging Face</strong> — type its name in the form{' '}
      <code>organisation/model</code> and choose Use. The settings link through to the{' '}
      <code>mlx-community</code> organisation, which converts most models to MLX.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A model folder</strong> — if Hugging Face isn’t reachable where you are, get an MLX
      model another way and choose <strong>Choose a Model Folder…</strong>. Once a model is on your
      device it works offline.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A model you already have</strong> — on a Mac, models downloaded with other MLX tools
      are in your Hugging Face cache, <code>~/.cache/huggingface/hub</code>. Choose the model’s own
      folder there, named like <code>models--mlx-community--…</code>, rather than a folder inside
      it; press <kbd>⇧⌘G</kbd> in the Open panel to type the path.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not every open model can use tools — Gemma 3, for one, has no way to be shown them. With such a
    model the AI settings say so, the Assistant chats without reading or changing your notes, and
    Research isn’t available; the writing tools work as usual. HelloNotes also says when a model’s
    weights are large for the device it is on.
  </p>
  <p>
    MLX runs on the device itself, so it is not available in the iOS Simulator.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">How much a model can read</h2>
  <p>
    Every model has a window — how much text it can hold at once. HelloNotes asks Apple
    Intelligence for its window rather than assuming one: 8,192 tokens on AFM 3 Core Advanced. An
    MLX model is given 16,384 tokens on a Mac and 8,192 on iPhone and iPad, because a larger window
    costs memory. Text in Chinese, Japanese or Korean takes about a token a character, so the same
    window holds fewer characters of it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A long note is summarised whole</strong> — in parts, then the parts — rather than by
      its opening.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rewrite asks for a shorter passage</strong> if a selection is more than the model can
      rewrite in one go, instead of rewriting only the part that fits and replacing all of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tags and links read a note’s opening</strong> if it is very long — they are
      suggestions — and tags come back in the note’s own language.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ManualLayout>
